  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLow-level mixing could allow for gusty westerly winds and \npotentially some marginal impacts across the interior. With gusts up \nto 30mph possible Thursday and Friday afternoon, some loose tree \nbranches could come down and unrestricted items like empty garbage \ncans and tents could get blown away.\n\nHigh pressure overhead Saturday night, in addition to calm \nwinds and mostly clear skies will allow for radiational cooling \nconditions. Low temperatures are likely to drop much further \nthan model guidance suggests. Low temperatures should range from\nupper 20s up north to mid-30s along the coast.\n\nTemperatures remain near seasonable to potentially mild through\nMonday with 850 mb temps around +11-12 C. This right now \nsupports highs into the 60s regionwide with upside for low-mid \n70s if strong surface heating can occur. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLatest forecast guidance continues to indicate that remnant moisture \nfrom Hurricane Isaias as well as an approaching front to our west \nwill bring a chance for rainfall to the area late Sunday through \nTuesday, possibly in two separate waves.\n\nStarting with the first wave, which will be the direct remnant \nmoisture plume from Isaias, it is expected to be captured by strong \nwesterly 300 mb flow and ejected eastward off the East Coast. Models \nhave come into agreement on this feature passing to our south Sunday \nnight-Monday morning, which is the primary window of opportunity for \nrainfall. Guidance continues to be split with a range of outcomes \nfrom mostly dry to a brief rain for the southern half of the \nforecast area, but totals generally are indicated to be at or below \nhalf an inch on most deterministic solutions with increasing \nPoPs with south extent.\n\nSome weak subsidence behind this first wave, so Monday is looking \nlike a dry day especially during the midday/afternoon hours. Some \nvigorous northern stream energy with a positively tilted trough will \nswing through Tuesday, bringing a separate opportunity for \nrain. Right now chances look more widespread then Sunday night, \nand the overall theme will be less moisture to work with but \nstronger forcing/dynamics. The highest PoPs will be in northern \nzones closer to the best jet dynamics, but high totals are \nunlikely given the progressive nature.\n\nMild nights are possible with slightly elevated dew points and \ngreater cloud cover early next week. 850 mb temps fall below 0 \nC by Wednesday behind a strong cold front, indicating cooler \ntemperatures are likely by mid week, and potentially a return of\ncrisp nights.",
